A||||23||Tonker||Want to learn about automechanic-y stuff...||||11||
Z||000000||Tonker||07-09-2001||06:51 PM||||So I'm a complete idiot when it comes to my car. You know, the old i press on the right pedal and the demons in the back make the car go faster, I press on the middle pedal and they grab onto the wheels to slow them down, etc. <P>Anyways I'm wondering if you guys have any suggestions on how I can learn about how this stuff actually works? (if it's demons so much the better) I'd also like to learn about how to work on my car, can you guys recommend any good beginner auto mechanic reference books?<P>AUUGGHH I'm soo useless when it comes to cars!||||reg||11
Z||000001||Steve Normington||07-09-2001||06:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tonker:<BR><B>Anyways I'm wondering if you guys have any suggestions on how I can learn about how this stuff actually works? (if it's demons so much the better) I'd also like to learn about how to work on my car, can you guys recommend any good beginner auto mechanic reference books?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I found the best way is to find someone in your area, buy him or her a case of beer and have them supervise while you mangle your car.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||07-09-2001||08:05 PM||||OR, just sit in here a couple of hours a day||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||07-09-2001||08:27 PM||||Physics, not demons; however, sometimes it's difficult to tell the difference.<P>I began seriously to learn about cars when I found that -- with a good manual, a few tools, and some patience -- I could repair my car better than the guys I was paying to do it.  So get a manual and a few tools, and try to understand how things work.  It's hard to fix something if you don't understand how it works when it ain't broke.<P>And occasionally it helps to sacrifice a chicken.  (Demons like chicken.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||07-09-2001||09:07 PM||||I bought a car when I was 15 that barely ran. I then dis-assembled the engine, attained a factory service manual and put it back together. Local shops and other mechanically inclined friends can also help.<P>Why is it later in life you always wished you kept your first car? No matter what kind of car it was or what shape it was in, you always want that car back.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||07-09-2001||09:34 PM||||Best thing for me was having a friend who knew everything about Fiero's help me out when I had a problem (mike).  I was a little hesitant at first but since I have installed starters, alternators, shocks, bushings, few other misc. mods.  Once you get the confidence the sky is the limit.||||reg||12
Z||000006||btoth||07-09-2001||11:51 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.howstuffworks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.howstuffworks.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||07-10-2001||12:40 AM||||All you need to know is that electronics work on smoke. When the smoke escapes, the part doesn't work anymore.<P>------------------<BR>Once we have all the bugs ironed out we'll be running on flat bugs.||||reg||1
Z||000008||The Fieromaster||07-10-2001||12:53 AM||||Not sure how old ya are bud, but check out auto mechanics in school... Get an idea for what it does... or help a friend do car work and bug the $hit outta him on every detail of what he is doing, why, and what it does when he does it... Your welcome to stop out and help me reasemble my gt  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      "Raced That Wrecked Better"<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif">     <BR>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000<BR>AIM: Fieromaster<BR>1985 GT ~Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)<BR>1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX (AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000||||reg||10
Z||000009||Tonker||07-10-2001||11:53 AM||||Thanks for the feedback guys.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I guess the main problem I have right now is that my car seems to be running nicely and anything I do to it is likely to change that for the worse. What I'd really like to do is get myself a cheap car and rip it apart. Mind you, living in an apartment won't make that easy...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>One of my friends has a Firebird and is somewhat mechanically inclined. Maybe he'll let me rip his car apart?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Or at least help...<P>btoth: That's an awsome site!! Gonna spend some time checking it out more fully!<P>I still think it's demons, and the chicken sacrifices will continue I'm proven wrong.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Black '86 Fiero GT.<BR>ICQ: 7813479||||reg||1
Z||000010||Formula88||07-10-2001||12:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Why is it later in life you always wished you kept your first car? No matter what kind of car it was or what shape it was in, you always want that car back.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Tell me about it!  I actually found my first car a couple of years ago (I sold it over 10 years ago).  It's sitting in some dude's backyard rotting.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  It's worth about $300, but he found out it was my first car, and wants $2500 for it.  As if!  Would have even considered it if it weren't for the fact the rear end was ripped out by someone driving over a stump when offroading.  Who the hell goes offroading in a 69 Firebird!?!?  He said he replaced it, but who knows what kind of unibody/suspension damage was done.  It's a crying shame.  That's when I got my Fiero.  So, I'm not feeling too bad about it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||6
Z||000011||Formula88||07-10-2001||12:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Best thing for me was having a friend who knew everything about Fiero's help me out when I had a problem (mike).  I was a little hesitant at first but since I have installed starters, alternators, shocks, bushings, few other misc. mods.  Once you get the confidence the sky is the limit.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Exactly!  I bought my Formula knowing it needed a clutch, only because LZeitgeist had done them before and was willing to help.  The first month I had the car, out comes the cradle and in goes a new clutch.  After that, I'm not afraid to work on ANYTHING on my Fiero!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Oh, and thanks again, LZ.  You're why I got a Fiero in the first place.  It's all YOUR fault!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000012||JohnnyK||07-10-2001||02:20 PM||||Buy a Fiero.. I knew nothing, and now I know tons (enough that I can fix most things on a car, and diagnose, etc).. Course, I also had my auto shop teacher helping me with some things.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||07-10-2001||02:39 PM||||The second thing you gotta know is mechanical parts are pumped full of noise<P>they are made in machine shops on equipment that pumps the metal full of noise<P>as the parts are used the noise gradually leaks out<P> and once all the noise is gone from the part - it stops working<P>  AND if all the noise leaks out all at once - you either have to take it back to a machine shop to have more noise pumped back into it - or buy a new one.<P> BTW - there are lots of places you can take auto repair courses  - community colleges and stuff - they got the tools and the shops and stuff - better than rebuilding a motor in your living room.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Philphine||07-10-2001||06:13 PM||||my first car was a '71 hornet. whoever has it can keep it.<P> my first half way serious car work was when my brakes needed work about 3 days after i lost my job. price quote, $300. most of what i had (part time job and in college). got a manual, bought the parts and worked at it about a week. cost about $100 and learned how to do brakes. since then i've called it desperation mechanics. i'm still no mr.goodwrench but i always get a manual for any car i get and try to figure it out before i give up and have it repaired. i get a little braver all the time.<P>pick up a chiltons, hanes, or shop manual (if you get into it you'll probaly end up with a couple of different manuals). do the really simple things like oil changes and minor tune-ups. just get your hands on it and notice other things under the hood. sort of like looking up words in a dictionary, you always notice a few other words and learn more than you meant to. it's even better in there's some more experienced people around to help. <P>another thing that helps me is now i can afford a couple of cars (no fleet of exotic's but you get the idea). i'm not pressed for time and if i think i can fix it i can drive another car while i work on it.<P>sorry. i started rambling. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>SERENITY NOW!!!!<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnnyK||07-10-2001||06:37 PM||||The great thing is it's not brain surgery, it's all common sense. If you can fix one disc brake, you can fix all of them (well, most, the ones like my honda accord are different because the caliper slides into a sort of "guard thing", but anyways, you get the idea.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fiero Sam||07-10-2001||06:37 PM||||There is a real good book called "Car Smarts"by Mary Jackson.There is also "Auto Repair For Dummies" by Deanna Sclar.I would suggest both of these books(start out with car smarts).When you think you have a grip on things,get a chilton or haynes manual for your car.I was a complete idiot when it came to cars a few months ago,these books really helped me.I agree with everyone else who says to help a friend work on their car.The trouble for me is that my friends know about as much as I did at the start.The ones who do know alot won't let me help.I am looking at going to classes to learn even more(that might cost money and time and I don't have alot of either).<BR>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000017||FieroLisa||07-10-2001||07:46 PM||||Fiero Sam: HA I have both those books  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> They are great for when you hear about something, and wanna know what it is/does... <BR>If I'm not mistaken, both those books were written by women.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Fiero Sam||07-11-2001||05:55 PM||||They were both written by women.They do tell alot about how things work.What happens when you do this and that,I think they are both pretty good.||||reg||1
Z||000019||MrPBody||07-11-2001||08:33 PM||||And there's Dick O'Kane's classic from the '60s: <I>How to Repair Your Foreign Car -- a guide for the beginner, your wife, and the mechanically inept</I>.  It's thoroughly out-of-date but entertaining.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||maryjane||07-18-2001||01:39 AM||||all good tips and advice! a few more:<BR> Don't take everything you read at face value-you'll have to learn to read between the lines when it comes to tech manuals.<BR> NEVER skip over the safety precautions. They're there for a reason. We've all gotten hurt,came close or damaged things because we became careless<BR> Buy the best tools you can afford. You don't have to buy off a Snap-on truck but quality tools won't let you down. Near the top of the list- a good set of jack stands,hand tools that won't strip out,duct tape (oops,too much red green show)<BR> Ask lots of questions, here & everywhere else. Taking something apart & putting it back together right is the easy part, troubleshooting problems is the hardest.<BR> Most of all- have fun!!<BR>Don <P>Ken, have ya really done a rebuild in your living room? I overhauled a bike eng in mine once, wife was NOT pleased!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||AusFiero||07-18-2001||12:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tonker:<BR><B>Thanks for the feedback guys.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>What I'd really like to do is get myself a cheap car and rip it apart. Mind you, living in an apartment won't make that easy...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No Not at all, you got a whole parking lot of cars to pull apart now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000022||Will||07-18-2001||10:18 PM||||Three rules:<P>1) righty-tighty, leftie-loosie<BR>2) put it back together the same way it came apart<BR>3) if you have parts left over when you're done you did it more efficiently than the engineers who designed it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||Formula||07-20-2001||08:47 PM||||I noticed that Will, Everytime I put stuff back together I get those extra bolts out that they put in there for either show or they might be emmisions bolts lol||||reg||1
